Event queue subsystem: faithful 16-bit DGROUP-offset reconstruction (enqueue/drain/init) + thread the
driver's c3fe button/cursor-move enqueue args -- unblocks the mouse event queue so a click reaches the
display event dispatch.

After patch 125 restores the enqueue vector c3fe = 349b, the button sub-handler 2f03 and the cursor-move
redraw 2f38 finally far-call the event enqueue FUN_1000_349b -- and it SEGV'd, because the whole event
queue was modeled with HOST POINTERS while the real code uses 16-bit DGROUP near-offset links.  The queue
is a free list of 63 18-byte (9-word) nodes at DGROUP:0x18ea; every link/handle is a 16-bit offset (empty
sentinel 0xffff).  The head/tail vars d8e0..d8e8 are ADJACENT DGROUP words, so Ghidra's 4-byte `int**`
typing (a) made d8e0 overlap d8e2, and (b) truncated every stored node link to 16 bits -> the second
enqueue read a garbage host pointer and faulted.  This path had never executed before (c3fe was corrupt),
so the model was never exercised.

FIX (asm-verified vs 0x13446 init, 0x1349b/0x134f5 enqueue, 0x13566 template-post, 0x135a7 drain):
- Retype d8e0/d8e2/d8e4/d8e8 (and keep d8e6) to 16-bit WORD offsets; add QOFF/QW helpers (offset->host).
- Reconstruct FUN_1000_3436/3446 (build the 63-node free list with 16-bit links, tail=&d8e4=0x18e4).
- Reconstruct FUN_1000_349b (append event to ready TAIL) and FUN_1000_34f5 (prepend to ready HEAD; now
  returns the node+2 handle in AX + CF via g_fist_cf, as its caller 3566 needs both).
- Reconstruct FUN_1000_3566 (post from the d8e0 template) and FUN_1000_35a7 (drain: call the node's
  callback [word7:word8], recycle the node to the free list, pop the ready head, publish d8e2=node+2).
- Update the d8e2 field readers (1e73/1e88 return word[1]; the 84xx drag block; 463f) to offset access.
- Thread the driver enqueue-vector args at 2f03 (AX = the button-transition bits param_1 & 6/0x18/0x60,
  DX = dd9e cursor-y; asm 0x2f0e..0x2f2f) and 2f38 (AX=1 cursor-move; asm 0x2f9b) so 349b receives the
  real event handle instead of dropped-arg garbage.
